[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Arrays, Slices, and Internals in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go provides two fundamental data structures for handling sequential data: arrays and slices. While arrays are fixed-size and value-based, slices are dynamic, flexible, and built on top of underlying arrays. Understanding their internal architecture is crucial for writing performant Go code and avoiding common memory leaks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Arrays in Go"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An array is a numbered sequence of elements of a specific length. In Go, arrays are values, meaning that assigning one array to another or passing an array to a function copies the entire array contents."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    // Declare an array of 3 integers (initialized to 0)
    var a [3]int
    fmt.Println("Default array:", a)

    // Declare and initialize using an array literal
    b := [3]int{1, 2, 3}
    fmt.Println("Initialized array:", b)

    // The size is part of the type. [3]int and [4]int are different types.
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Slices and Their Internal Structure"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Slices are much more common in Go than arrays. A slice is a descriptor of a contiguous segment of an underlying array and provides access to a numbered sequence of elements from that array. Internally, a slice is represented by a small data structure called a slice header, which contains three fields:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pointer: A pointer pointing to the first element of the underlying array that is reachable from the slice."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Length: The number of elements present in the slice. Accessed via len(s)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Capacity: The maximum number of elements the slice can contain without allocating a new underlying array (length of the underlying array from the slice pointer). Accessed via cap(s)."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    // Creating a slice using make
    // make([]T, length, capacity)
    s := make([]int, 3, 5)
    fmt.Printf("Len: %d, Cap: %d, Val: %v\n", len(s), cap(s), s)
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Slice Operations and Memory Allocation"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you append elements to a slice using the built-in append function, Go checks if the current capacity is sufficient. If the capacity is exceeded, Go automatically allocates a new, larger underlying array (typically doubling the capacity for small slices), copies the old elements to the new array, and returns the new slice header."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    var s []int // nil slice, len = 0, cap = 0

    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        s = append(s, i)
        fmt.Printf("Len: %d, Cap: %d\n", len(s), cap(s))
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Pitfalls: Memory Leaks with Slices"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because a slice references an underlying array, a small slice keeping reference to a massive underlying array can prevent the garbage collector from freeing that large array from memory. To prevent this, you can copy the required elements into a new slice."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"expertSlice := make([]int, 1000000)
// If we only need a small portion and want to release the rest:
smallSlice := make([]int, 10)
copy(smallSlice, expertSlice[0:10])
// expertSlice underlying array can now be garbage collected if no other references exist"}]
